Project Blueprint — Version 1.0
Product definition · Foundation before development

We're going to treat this as a real software product blueprint, not simply a list of features to feed into an AI coding tool.

The blueprint will define the product identity, vision, user experience, functional scope, screens, architecture direction, data relationships, AI boundaries, build phases and development rules.

01 — Product identity
Working name: Daywell
Plan intentionally. Live consistently. Think clearly.

Daywell is a provisional name for the blueprint. We haven't checked trademark, domain or naming availability.
DAYWELL
A personal life management platform.

Plan your work, build your habits, track your progress and use intelligent assistance to make your everyday life more manageable.

PLAN


LIVE


THINK




Product philosophy
1. One unified space. Avoid fragmented productivity tools.
2. Consistency over perfection. Progress matters more than perfect streaks.
3. Function before AI. Every core feature must work independently of AI.
4. AI with purpose. AI should solve meaningful planning and decision problems.
5. Visual progress. Make progress tangible, not hidden inside statistics.
6. User control. AI proposes; the user controls meaningful changes.
7. Minimal friction. Logging a habit or completing a task should be effortless.

02 — The product vision
Daywell helps people manage three connected aspects of their lives.
PLAN

Manage your commitments.

Organize tasks, goals, projects and schedules. Know what needs to be done and when.

LIVE

Build the life you want to maintain.

Track habits, exercise, routines, consistency and progress over time.

THINK

Make sense of it all.

An intelligent assistant that understands the context of your plans and habits to offer insights and help you take action.


The core user experience
The user opens Daywell in the morning.

They see:
- What matters today.
- What their schedule looks like.
- The habits they need to maintain.
- Their progress.
- Their goals.
- What needs attention.
During the day, they complete tasks and log habits with minimal friction.

Over time, they can view their consistency, activity, milestones and progress.

When they feel overwhelmed, uncertain or want to optimize their plans, they consult THINK.

Daywell should make the user feel organized, motivated and in control, not guilty about unfinished tasks.

